Use this prompt:

```text
Refactor my existing Microsoft Fabric Lakehouse PySpark notebook into clearly segregated notebook cells.

GOAL
- Split the entire notebook into separate cells by responsibility/function.
- Each cell must be able to run on its own after its prerequisite cells have run.
- If I use Run All, cells must execute successfully one after another in order.
- Do NOT change business logic unless required for clean cell boundaries.
- Do NOT merge everything into one giant cell.
- Keep Landing -> Bronze behavior intact (all-string Bronze, Excel to Exceptions, multi-client load, and any added hashing/idempotency/versioning/validation/audit logic if present).

CELL DESIGN RULES
1) One purpose per cell.
2) Put shared CONFIG in its own top cell.
3) Put shared imports in its own cell.
4) Put helper functions in one or more dedicated cells (no heavy execution there except def/class).
5) Put Control table DDL / ensure-tables in its own cell.
6) Put discovery, hashing, decision (idempotency/versioning), read/transform, validation, Bronze write, audit/logging, Delta history, and summary each in separate cells where applicable.
7) Every cell should start with a short markdown-style comment header:
   # CELL N — <Name>
   # Purpose: ...
   # Depends on: CELL ...
   # Outputs: ...
8) Avoid hidden order bugs:
   - define variables before use
   - create tables before writing
   - define functions before calling
9) Make Run All safe:
   - idempotent setup cells (CREATE SCHEMA/TABLE IF NOT EXISTS)
   - clear status prints after each major cell
10) If a cell is optional (e.g., Delta history demo), mark it OPTIONAL but keep it runnable.
11) Do not require manual copy-paste between cells during a normal run; cells should use notebook-scoped variables/functions.
12) Preserve existing folder conventions:
   - Files/Landing_zone/<client>/
   - Files/Exceptions/<client>/Excel/
   - Files/Quarantine/<client>/ (if used)
   - Files/Archive/<client>/... (if used)

REQUESTED CELL BREAKDOWN (use this structure; adjust names only if needed)
Cell 0 — Markdown: Notebook overview + run order
Cell 1 — Imports
Cell 2 — CONFIG (paths, schemas, extensions, defaults, overrides)
Cell 3 — Helper functions (path/list/move/sanitize/read/hash/decision helpers)
Cell 4 — Ensure schemas/folders
Cell 5 — Ensure Control tables (file_processing_control, ingestion_audit, etc.)
Cell 6 — Discover client folders + files
Cell 7 — File hashing
Cell 8 — Idempotency + file version decision
Cell 9 — Validation + duplicate checks (Bronze-level)
Cell 10 — Quarantine / Excel exception handling actions
Cell 11 — Read + transform to all-string + technical columns
Cell 12 — Write Bronze Delta tables (APPEND for versioned loads)
Cell 13 — Update control/audit statuses (SUCCESS/SKIPPED/FAILED)
Cell 14 — Delta table versioning/history demo (DESCRIBE HISTORY / time travel examples)
Cell 15 — Final summary/display

OUTPUT FORMAT
1) Give the exact recommended cell order as a checklist.
2) Produce the full notebook content cell by cell.
   - For each cell, clearly label:
     ### CELL N — Name
     then the complete code for that cell.
3) For each cell, include:
   - Purpose
   - Depends on
   - What it outputs/creates
4) Call out any tiny hook changes needed so cells work sequentially.
5) Keep comments concise and practical.
6) Ensure that running CELL1 → CELL15 in order works, and also Run All works.

CONSTRAINTS
- Fabric Lakehouse notebook + PySpark + mssparkutils
- Additive/refactor for structure; do not invent unrelated features
- Bronze business columns remain string
- File version (INT) and Delta table version (BIGINT) must remain separate fields/types
- Clear handling notes if a later cell depends on lists/dataframes created earlier (e.g., files_df, decisions_df, batch_id)

Return only the segregated notebook cells and the short run-order guide.
```
